    <link-summary>Configure feed registration models and feed query chunk size.</link-summary>
    <card-summary>Configure feed registration models and feed query chunk size.</card-summary>
    <web-summary>Configure feed registration models and feed query chunk size.</web-summary>

    <show-structure depth="3" />

    <chapter title="Custom feed registration model" id="custom_feed_model">
        <p>
            Set <code>feeds.model</code> to an application model when feed registration records need additional
            columns, casts, relationships, or methods. The custom model must extend
            <code>DragonCode\LaravelFeed\Models\Feed</code>.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="php">
            namespace App\Models;

            use DragonCode\LaravelFeed\Models\Feed as BaseFeed;

            class Feed extends BaseFeed
            {
                protected $fillable = [
                    'class',
                    'title',
                    'expression',
                    'is_active',
                    'last_activity',
                    'is_available_fulfilment',
                    'is_available_omni',
                    'is_available_sfs',
                ];

                protected function casts(): array
                {
                    return [
                        ...parent::casts(),
                        'is_available_fulfilment' =&gt; 'boolean',
                        'is_available_omni' =&gt; 'boolean',
                        'is_available_sfs' =&gt; 'boolean',
                    ];
                }
            }
        </code-block>

        <p>Register the model in <path>config/feeds.php</path>:</p>

        <code-block lang="php">
            'model' =&gt; App\Models\Feed::class,
        </code-block>

        <p>
            Add the custom columns to the feeds table in an application migration. Keep every attribute accepted by
            mass assignment in <code>$fillable</code>.
        </p>

        <p>
            <code>FeedQuery</code> uses the configured model for create, find, list, update, delete, and restore
            operations. Pass application-specific values through <code>extra</code> when creating a record. Explicit
            method arguments take precedence over duplicate keys in <code>extra</code>:
        </p>

        <code-block lang="php">
            $feed = app(FeedQuery::class)-&gt;create(
                class: SomeFeed::class,
                title: 'Some',
                expression: '0 */12 * * *',
                extra: [
                    'is_available_fulfilment' =&gt; true,
                    'is_available_omni' =&gt; true,
                    'is_available_sfs' =&gt; true,
                ]
            );
        </code-block>

        <p>The application model can also use the regular Eloquent API:</p>

        <code-block lang="php">
            use App\Models\Feed;

            $feed = Feed::create([
                'class' =&gt; SomeFeed::class,
                'title' =&gt; 'Some',
                'expression' =&gt; '0 */12 * * *',
                'is_active' =&gt; true,
                'is_available_fulfilment' =&gt; true,
                'is_available_omni' =&gt; true,
                'is_available_sfs' =&gt; true,
            ]);
        </code-block>

        <note>
            The configured model is resolved at runtime. Static analysis sees the result of
            <code>FeedQuery::create()</code> as the package base model. Use <code>App\Models\Feed::create()</code> or
            an explicit local type when autocomplete for application-specific properties is required.
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Chunk size" id="chunk_size">
